in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I take a look at a new networking  algorithm that gives your clients a big  performance win is this tech too good to  be true  stay tuned to find out if you follow  along with our content you probably know  that we never advocate for a silver  bullet when it comes to performance  there's always a horde of trade-offs  that you have to consider before things  make sense for your scenario but I'm  going to break my rule this time and say  that you should really be using a new  networking algorithm called TCP bbr  which can give your clients in poor  connectivity areas as much as a 2x boost  in performance and all you have to do is  set a simple server-side flag see TCP as  a protocol tries it's best to balance  between being fast and being fair with  much more emphasis on the being fair  part for example as packet loss on your  network increases TCPS back off  algorithm will reduce the performance of  each user so that everyone can share the  pipe but fine-tuning this balance is  really tricky with TCP s current back  off algorithms even if you're the only  person using the pipe it can under  utilize your network connection by as  much as 50 percent this is where TCP bbr  comes in it's an updated back off  algorithm that's designed to respond to  actual congestion rather than just  simple packet loss which means that it's  focused on improving network performance  in the worst case scenario when your  network isn't very good now sadly I  don't have access to a consistently  unstable Network so for the sake of our  tests we're going to simulate some  worst-case scenarios to test things I  set up two VMs in two different regions  one to be the client and one to be to  server and did a quick iperf test to  check out their performance then to  simulate our unstable Network we run the  following TC command on the client which  will reduce performance by increasing  packet loss re running the tests we see  that this has a dramatic 